          'content': 'WHAT IS AN ANALOGY?\nAn analogy establishes a relationship between two pairs of words. The key is to identify the EXACT nature of the relationship in the first pair and find the same relationship in the answer options.\n\nTYPES OF ANALOGIES:\n\n1. SYNONYM ANALOGY\nWords have similar meaning.\nExample: Happy : Joyful :: Sad : Sorrowful\nBig : Large :: Small : Tiny\n\n2. ANTONYM ANALOGY\nWords have opposite meaning.\nExample: Day : Night :: Light : Dark\nHot : Cold :: Fast : Slow\n\n3. PART TO WHOLE\nFirst word is part of the second.\nExample: Wheel : Car :: Petal : Flower\nChapter : Book :: Branch : Tree\nPage : Novel :: Brick : Wall\n\n4. WHOLE TO PART\nFirst word contains the second.\nExample: Car : Wheel :: Flower : Petal\nArmy : Soldier :: Forest : Tree\n\n5. TOOL TO FUNCTION\nFirst word is used to do the second.\nExample: Pen : Write :: Knife : Cut\nBrush : Paint :: Needle : Sew\nScalpel : Operate :: Hammer : Nail\n\n6. WORKER TO TOOL\nExample: Carpenter : Saw :: Painter : Brush\nDoctor : Stethoscope :: Soldier : Gun\nFarmer : Plough :: Writer : Pen\n\n7. WORKER TO PRODUCT\nExample: Baker : Bread :: Potter : Pot\nAuthor : Book :: Sculptor : Statue\nWeaver : Cloth :: Carpenter : Furniture\n\n8. ANIMAL TO YOUNG ONE\nExample: Cow : Calf :: Horse : Foal\nDog : Puppy :: Cat : Kitten\nFrog : Tadpole :: Butterfly : Caterpillar\nDeer : Fawn :: Lion : Cub\nSheep : Lamb :: Goat : Kid\n\n9. ANIMAL TO SOUND\nExample: Dog : Bark :: Cat : Meow\nLion : Roar :: Snake : Hiss\nFrog : Croak :: Bee : Buzz\nCow : Moo :: Duck : Quack\nElephant : Trumpet :: Horse : Neigh\n\n10. ANIMAL TO HABITAT\nExample: Lion : Den :: Bird : Nest\nBee : Hive :: Rabbit : Burrow\nFish : Water :: Eagle : Sky\nHorse : Stable :: Dog : Kennel\n\n11. COUNTRY TO CAPITAL\nExample: India : New Delhi :: France : Paris\nJapan : Tokyo :: Germany : Berlin\nAustralia : Canberra :: Canada : Ottawa\nBrazil : Brasilia :: Russia : Moscow\n\n12. COUNTRY TO CURRENCY\nExample: India : Rupee :: USA : 