          'difficulty': 'Medium',
          'readTime': '15 mins',
          'content': 'WHAT IS SYLLOGISM?\nSyllogism involves drawing logical conclusions from given statements using Venn diagrams. The conclusion must NECESSARILY follow from the statements — not just possibly.\n\nTYPES OF STATEMENTS:\n1. Universal Affirmative (A): ALL A are B\n2. Universal Negative (E): NO A is B\n3. Particular Affirmative (I): SOME A are B\n4. Particular Negative (O): SOME A are not B\n\nIMPORTANT RULES:\n\nRule 1: ALL A are B → does NOT mean ALL B are A\nExample: All cats are animals → true\nBut: All animals are cats → FALSE\n\nRule 2: ALL A are B → SOME A are B is ALWAYS true\n(If all are B, then some are definitely B)\n\nRule 3: ALL A are B + ALL B are C = ALL A are C ✅\nExample: All dogs are animals. All animals are living.\n→ All dogs are living. ✅\n\nRule 4: ALL A are B + NO B is C = NO A is C ✅\nExample: All pens are books. No book is pencil.\n→ No pen is pencil. ✅\n\nRule 5: SOME A are B + ALL B are C = SOME A are C ✅\nExample: Some cats are dogs. All dogs are animals.\n→ Some cats are animals. ✅\n\nRule 6: SOME A are B + NO B is C = SOME A are NOT C ✅\nExample: Some pens are books. No book is pencil.\n→ Some pens are not pencils. ✅\n\nRule 7: SOME A are B + SOME B are C = NO definite conclusion\n(Just because some A are B and some B are C doesn\'t mean any A is C)\n\nVENN DIAGRAM METHOD (Most Reliable):\n\nFor \'All A are B\': Draw circle A completely INSIDE circle B\nFor \'No A is B\': Draw circles A and B COMPLETELY SEPARATE\nFor \'Some A are B\': Draw circles A and B PARTIALLY OVERLAPPING\nFor \'Some A are not B\': Draw circle A partially outside B\n\nSteps:\n1. Draw all possible Venn diagrams for given statements\n2. Check if conclusion holds in ALL possible diagrams\n3. Only mark conclusion TRUE if it holds in EVERY diagram\n\nCOMPLEMENTARY PAIRS (Either-Or type):\nIf one conclusion says \'Some A are B\' and another says \'No A is B\',\nand neither is directly provable, they form a complementary pair.\nAnswer: Either conclusion 1 or conclusion 2 follows.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\n1. Confusing \'possible\' with \'definite\' — conclusion must be DEFINITE\n2. Reversing universal statements (All A=B ≠ All B=A)\n3. Forgetting that \'All A are B\' also implies \'Some A are B\'\n4. Not checking ALL possible Venn diagram arrangements\n\nEXAM TIPS:\n• Use Venn diagrams for every question — don\'t rely on intuition\n• Time: 60-90 seconds per question\n• SSC CGL usually gives 2 statements and 2-3 conclusions to evaluate',
