          'content': 'TENSES (Complete Guide):\n\nPRESENT:\n• Simple Present: I eat / He eats (habit/fact)\n• Present Continuous: I am eating (happening now)\n• Present Perfect: I have eaten (completed, effect now)\n• Present Perfect Continuous: I have been eating (started past, continuing)\n\nPAST:\n• Simple Past: I ate (completed action)\n• Past Continuous: I was eating (was happening)\n• Past Perfect: I had eaten (before another past action)\n• Past Perfect Continuous: I had been eating (was happening before another past action)\n\nFUTURE:\n• Simple Future: I will eat\n• Future Continuous: I will be eating\n• Future Perfect: I will have eaten\n• Future Perfect Continuous: I will have been eating\n\nSUBJECT-VERB AGREEMENT RULES:\n1. Each, Every, Either, Neither, Anyone, Everybody → SINGULAR verb\n   \'Each of the students HAS a book\' ✓\n   \'Each of the students HAVE a book\' ✗\n\n2. Or/Nor with mixed subjects → verb agrees with NEARER subject\n   \'Neither the teacher nor the students WERE present\' ✓\n   \'Either he or I AM responsible\' ✓\n\n3. Collective nouns → SINGULAR (army, jury, committee, team)\n   \'The jury HAS given its verdict\' ✓\n   BUT if acting as individuals → plural\n   \'The jury ARE divided in opinion\' ✓\n\n4. \'The number of\' → SINGULAR, \'A number of\' → PLURAL\n   \'The number of students IS large\' ✓\n   \'A number of students ARE absent\' ✓\n\n5. Mathematics, Physics, News (ending in -s but singular) → singular verb\n   \'Mathematics IS difficult\' ✓\n   \'The news IS good\' ✓\n\nARTICLES (A, An, The):\n• A: before consonant sounds (a dog, a union — \'u\' sounds like \'y\')\n• An: before vowel sounds (an apple, an hour — \'h\' is silent)\n• The: specific/known to both, superlatives, unique things\n  The sun, the moon, the Prime Minister, the Ganga\n• No article: languages (Hindi), sports, meals (breakfast), proper nouns\n\nPREPOSITIONS:\n• At: specific time (at 5 PM, at midnight), specific place (at the door)\n• In: longer periods (in March, in 2026), enclosed spaces (in the box)\n• On: days/dates (on Monday, on 15th August), surfaces (on the table)\n• For: duration (for 3 hours), purpose (for you)\n• Since: point of time (since Monday, since 2020) — with perfect tenses\n• By: not later than (by tomorrow), near (by the river)\n• With: accompaniment (with friends), instrument (cut with knife)\n\nCOMMON ERRORS:\n• Fewer (countable: fewer students) vs Less (uncountable: less water)\n• Number (countable: number of books) vs Amount (uncountable: amount of water)\n• Between (two items) vs Among (more than two)\n• Bring (towards speaker) vs Take (away from speaker)\n• Lend (give temporarily) vs Borrow (receive temporarily)\n• Lay (transitive — place something) vs Lie (intransitive — recline)\n• Raise (transitive — lift something) vs Rise (intransitive — go up)\n• Hanged (person — capital punishment) vs Hung (object — picture hung)\n• Affect (verb — influence) vs Effect (noun — result)\n• Principal (head/main) vs Principle (rule/belief)',
