          'content': 'READING COMPREHENSION STRATEGY:\n\nSTEP 1 — READ QUESTIONS FIRST\nBefore reading the passage, scan the questions quickly.\nKnowing what to look for saves time and improves accuracy.\n\nSTEP 2 — SKIM THE PASSAGE\nRead the first and last paragraph carefully.\nRead only the first sentence of middle paragraphs.\nThis gives you the main idea in 1 minute.\n\nSTEP 3 — ANSWER DIRECTLY STATED QUESTIONS FIRST\nSome questions have direct answers in the text.\nFind these quickly using keywords from the question.\n\nSTEP 4 — INFERENCE QUESTIONS\nThe answer is not directly stated but can be deduced.\nStay close to what the passage says — don\'t over-assume.\n\nTYPES OF QUESTIONS:\n\n1. DIRECT/FACTUAL: \'According to the passage...\'\n   Answer is explicitly stated in text. Scan for keywords.\n\n2. INFERENCE: \'It can be inferred that...\'\n   Answer is implied but not stated. Choose the closest logical inference.\n\n3. MAIN IDEA/THEME: \'The passage is mainly about...\'\n   Look at what MOST of the passage discusses, not just one part.\n   The correct answer covers the ENTIRE passage scope.\n\n4. TITLE QUESTION: \'The best title for the passage would be...\'\n   Must capture the main idea without being too broad or too narrow.\n\n5. VOCABULARY IN CONTEXT: \'As used in paragraph 2, X means...\'\n   Don\'t use your pre-existing knowledge — use the CONTEXT.\n   Read the sentence before and after the word.\n\n6. AUTHOR\'S TONE/ATTITUDE:\n   Critical: author finds fault\n   Appreciative/Laudatory: author praises\n   Nostalgic: author longs for the past\n   Sarcastic: says opposite of what is meant\n   Objective/Neutral: presents facts without bias\n   Persuasive: tries to convince reader\n\n7. PURPOSE: \'The author mentions X in order to...\'\n   Think: why would the author include this detail?\n\nGOLDEN RULES:\n1. Answer must be SUPPORTED by the passage — no outside knowledge\n2. Too specific or too general options are usually wrong\n3. Extreme words (always, never, all, none) are usually wrong\n4. \'Closest in meaning\' = read context, not dictionary meaning\n5. Wrong options are usually: too broad, too narrow, opposite, or not mentioned\n\nTIME MANAGEMENT:\n• 3-4 questions per passage, 1 passage typically\n• Spend max 5 minutes on one passage\n• If stuck on inference, eliminate clearly wrong options first',
