          'content': 'ERROR SPOTTING — COMMON CATEGORIES:\n\n1. SUBJECT-VERB AGREEMENT ERRORS:\nWrong: Each of the boys have done their homework.\nRight: Each of the boys HAS done their homework. (Each = singular)\n\nWrong: The committee have taken a decision.\nRight: The committee HAS taken a decision. (Collective noun = singular)\n\n2. TENSE ERRORS:\nWrong: He has left the city yesterday.\nRight: He LEFT the city yesterday. (Yesterday = specific past, use simple past)\n\nWrong: If he will come, I will tell him.\nRight: If he COMES, I will tell him. (Conditional: present in if-clause)\n\n3. PRONOUN ERRORS:\nWrong: Everyone should do their best. (Technically singular)\nRight: Everyone should do his/her best.\n\nWrong: It is me who is responsible.\nRight: It is I who am responsible.\n(After \'It is/was\', use subject pronouns: I, he, she, we, they)\n\n4. ARTICLE ERRORS:\nWrong: She is a honest woman.\nRight: She is AN honest woman. (\'H\' is silent = vowel sound)\n\nWrong: He is best student in class.\nRight: He is THE best student in class. (Superlative needs \'the\')\n\n5. PREPOSITION ERRORS:\nWrong: I have been working here since three years.\nRight: I have been working here FOR three years. (Duration = for)\n\nWrong: He is angry on me.\nRight: He is angry WITH me. (angry with a person)\n\n6. DOUBLE NEGATIVE (avoid):\nWrong: I don\'t know nothing.\nRight: I don\'t know anything. / I know nothing.\n\n7. REDUNDANCY:\nWrong: Please revert back to me.\nRight: Please revert to me. (\'Revert\' already means \'back\')\n\nWrong: He is the most senior-most officer.\nRight: He is the most senior officer. (\'Most\' + \'-most\' = redundant)\n\nSENTENCE IMPROVEMENT — KEY RULES:\n\nRule 1: Use \'as well as\' — verb agrees with FIRST subject\n\'The teacher as well as students HAS come.\'\n\nRule 2: \'Neither...nor\' / \'Either...or\' — verb agrees with NEARER subject\n\'Neither he nor I AM responsible.\'\n\nRule 3: \'One of\' + plural noun + singular verb\n\'One of the boys HAS won the prize.\'\n\nRule 4: Gerund after prepositions\n\'She insisted on GOING.\' (not \'to go\' after preposition)\n\nRule 5: \'Used to\' vs \'Be used to\'\n\'I used to play cricket.\' (past habit)\n\'I am used to playing cricket.\' (accustomed to)\n\nCOMMON CONFUSED PAIRS:\n• Accept (receive) vs Except (exclude)\n• Advice (noun) vs Advise (verb)\n• Desert (abandon/dry land) vs Dessert (sweet)\n• Elicit (draw out) vs Illicit (illegal)\n• Eminent (famous) vs Imminent (about to happen)\n• Flair (talent) vs Flare (flame/spread)\n• Historic (significant) vs Historical (of history)',
