          'content': 'MAKING OF THE CONSTITUTION:\n• Constituent Assembly: Set up under Cabinet Mission Plan 1946\n• First meeting: 9 December 1946\n• Dr. Rajendra Prasad: President of Constituent Assembly\n• Dr. B.R. Ambedkar: Chairman of Drafting Committee\n  — Called \'Father of Indian Constitution\'\n• B.N. Rau: Constitutional Adviser\n• Adopted: 26 November 1949 (Constitution Day)\n• Enforced/Effective: 26 January 1950 (Republic Day)\n• Time taken: 2 years, 11 months, 18 days\n• Sessions held: 11 sessions\n• Original: 395 Articles, 8 Schedules, 22 Parts\n• Currently: 448 Articles, 12 Schedules, 25 Parts\n\nSOURCES OF CONSTITUTION:\n• UK: Parliamentary system, Rule of Law, Cabinet system, Writs\n• USA: Fundamental Rights, Judicial Review, Preamble\n• Ireland: DPSP, Emergency provisions, Nomination of RS members\n• Canada: Federal structure with strong Centre, Governor, Supreme Court\n• Germany (Weimar): Emergency provisions\n• South Africa: Amendment procedure (Part of Sch 15)\n• USSR: Fundamental Duties, 5-year plans\n• France: Liberty, Equality, Fraternity (in Preamble)\n• Australia: Joint sitting of Parliament, Concurrent list\n• Japan: Due process of law\n\nPREAMBLE:\nWE, THE PEOPLE OF INDIA, having solemnly resolved to constitute India into a SOVEREIGN SOCIALIST SECULAR DEMOCRATIC REPUBLIC and to secure to all its citizens:\nJUSTICE (social, economic, political)\nLIBERTY (of thought, expression, belief, faith and worship)\nEQUALITY (of status and opportunity)\nFRATERNITY (assuring dignity of individual and unity/integrity of nation)\n\n• \'Socialist\' and \'Secular\' added by 42nd Amendment 1976\n• Preamble is NOT enforceable in court but is part of Constitution\n\nFUNDAMENTAL RIGHTS (Part III, Art 12-35):\n1. Right to Equality (Art 14-18)\n   Art 14: Equality before law\n   Art 15: No discrimination on race, religion, sex, caste, place of birth\n   Art 16: Equal opportunity in public employment\n   Art 17: Abolition of untouchability (punishable offense)\n   Art 18: Abolition of titles (except military/academic)\n\n2. Right to Freedom (Art 19-22)\n   Art 19: 6 freedoms — Speech, Assembly, Association, Movement, Residence, Profession\n   Art 20: Protection in conviction\n   Art 21: Right to Life and Personal Liberty\n   Art 21A: Right to Education (6-14 years) — added by 86th Amendment 2002\n   Art 22: Protection against arbitrary arrest\n\n3. Right Against Exploitation (Art 23-24)\n   Art 23: Prohibition of trafficking and forced labour\n   Art 24: Prohibition of child labour (under 14 in factories/hazardous)\n\n4. Right to Freedom of Religion (Art 25-28)\n   Art 25: Freedom of conscience and religion\n   Art 26: Freedom to manage religious affairs\n   Art 28: No religious instruction in state-funded schools\n\n5. Cultural and Educational Rights (Art 29-30)\n   Art 29: Protection of interests of minorities\n   Art 30: Right of minorities to establish educational institutions\n\n6. Right to Constitutional Remedies (Art 32)\n   \'Heart and Soul of the Constitution\' — Dr. Ambedkar\n   Five writs: Habeas Corpus, Mandamus, Prohibition, Certiorari, Quo Warranto\n\nDIRECTIVE PRINCIPLES OF STATE POLICY (Part IV, Art 36-51):\n• Non-justiciable (cannot be enforced in court)\n• Inspired by Irish Constitution\n• Positive obligations on state\n• Key DPSPs: Equal pay for equal work, free legal aid, UCC, Panchayati Raj\n\nFUNDAMENTAL DUTIES (Part IVA, Art 51A):\n• Added by 42nd Amendment 1976\n• Originally 10 duties, 11th added by 86th Amendment 2002\n• Not justiciable but morally binding\n• Important duties: Respect Constitution, National Anthem/Flag, Defend country\n\nPARLIAMENT:\n• Lok Sabha: Lower house, maximum 552 members, 5-year term\n• Rajya Sabha: Upper house, maximum 250 members, permanent (1/3 retire every 2 years)\n• Money Bill can only be introduced in Lok Sabha\n• Joint sitting: In case of deadlock (presided by Lok Sabha Speaker)\n\nSUPREME COURT:\n• Established: 26 January 1950\n• Original, appellate, advisory jurisdiction\n• Guardian of Constitution, protector of Fundamental Rights\n• Can issue writs under Art 32',
